"Amplifying African Knowledge in the Digital Age" — Muizenberg, South Africa (July 2026)

In July 2026, I volunteered to participate in the Wikimedia Edit-a-Thon workshop series, organized by the AIMS South Africa House of Science, under the direction of Dr. Rejoyce Gavhi-Molefe (House of Science Manager), with the support of Tochi Precious (co-founder of the Igbo Wikimedians User Group and global coordinator of the Wikimedia Language Diversity Hub) and Eugene Agbor Egbe (researcher at Paderborn University, Germany).

This project aims to address the underrepresentation of African scientists on Wikipedia, one of the world's most influential digital platforms. The initiative primarily focuses on African female and male mathematicians, by creating and improving their Wikipedia articles, in order to preserve and amplify the visibility of African scientific knowledge in global digital knowledge systems.

My contribution: as a participant, I took part in the first session of the workshop (July 11, 2026, AIMS Main Lab), dedicated to discovering the Wikimedia ecosystem, creating a Wikipedia account, and making initial guided modifications ("edits"). The program also involved:
- nominating at least four notable African mathematical scientists (including at least three women), originating from my home country or the African continent;
- conducting documentary research to verify the notability of these figures based on reliable and published sources;
- training in Wikipedia editing, academic writing, and science communication;
- contributing to the creation of Wikipedia profiles unveiled during an international event celebrating mathematics and women in science.
